WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.950
What's the biggest problem facing
New York, or London, or San Fransisco?

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.560
Many people would say
the high cost of housing.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.200
As cities have become safer and

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.730
more economically successful,
they have also become more expensive.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.200
Sky high real estate prices seem to be
turning many cities from places where

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.060
ordinary people can find a better future
to boutique towns that are affordable

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.670
only to the already prosperous.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.470
The housing problem afflicts not
just the wealthiest cities, but

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.440
also some of the poorest ones as well.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.400
Yes, living in Johannesburg is
cheaper than living in New York, but

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.250
the cost of housing can loom large to
an economically desperate South African.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.520
When we think about creating
affordable housing,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.370
we need to remember that we need
solutions for both Boston and Calcutta.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.790
And those solutions
are unlikely to be the same.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.410
The first question this section
asks is why is housing so

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.830
much more expensive is
some places than others?

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.900
Are high prices driven by robust demand,
or limited supply, or both?

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.090
And will the high housing prices that
we see in cities like Shanghai and

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.170
San Francisco persist?

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.880
Buying a house is often the biggest
financial decision that a person makes.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.040
It seems sensible to think a bit
about what keeps prices high.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.540
The second question is what should be
done to make cities more affordable?

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.070
Does the affordability require
rent control or housing vouchers?

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.310
Should we be subsidizing new construction
or adding units of public housing?

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.310
And who's gonna pay for all these parties?

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.540
Restricting the supply of the new
housing is never going to make housing

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.750
less expensive, but

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.890
that doesn't mean that government
shouldn't regulate new housing at all.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.580
What if all the new buildings just seem
to be apartments for billionaires?

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.210
Should London restrict heights to preserve
sight lines at St Paul's cathedral?

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.140
Should Boston stop building to keep
shadows from falling on urban parks?

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.570
Should cities preserve old buildings
as a reminder of our urban past?

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.200
Too many great cities have
become too expensive.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.740
We need to understand why and
what can be done about it.

